1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:02,537
This fell on my pillow on my first night.

2
00:00:02,650 --> 00:00:04,512
It is a piece of my bedroom wall.

3
00:00:04,625 --> 00:00:07,437
I walked past my hotel
twice before I found it.

4
00:00:07,550 --> 00:00:08,775
I was standing on it.

5
00:00:08,888 --> 00:00:10,325
Hi, I'm Wob.

6
00:00:10,438 --> 00:00:13,287
I visit odd places and
ask why anyone stays.

7
00:00:13,850 --> 00:00:17,662
The town is Matmata, in the
dry hills of southern Tunisia,

8
00:00:17,712 --> 00:00:21,300
about three hundred and sixty-five
kilometres south of Tunis.

9
00:00:21,412 --> 00:00:23,838
That's around two hundred
and twenty-five miles.

10
00:00:23,950 --> 00:00:26,725
From the road, I couldn't
see a single house.

11
00:00:26,838 --> 00:00:29,225
I went to find out who would
choose to live like that,

12
00:00:29,312 --> 00:00:30,350
and whether I could.

13
00:00:30,462 --> 00:00:31,900
I even packed to stay.

14
00:00:32,012 --> 00:00:35,387
A sun hat, sunscreen, and a very big bag.

15
00:00:35,500 --> 00:00:36,537
No umbrella.

16
00:00:36,650 --> 00:00:37,750
Why would I need one?

17
00:00:37,863 --> 00:00:41,050
By the end of the trip, I knew
who really runs the place.

18
00:00:41,163 --> 00:00:42,087
It wasn't me.

19
00:00:42,200 --> 00:00:43,625
It wasn't the sun either.

20
00:00:43,738 --> 00:00:47,175
Somewhere in this story, a camel
makes a delivery through a roof.

21
00:00:47,288 --> 00:00:49,000
I'll show you on a tray of sand.

22
00:00:49,576 --> 00:00:51,014
I arrived in October.

23
00:00:51,126 --> 00:00:54,288
I got out of the car in old
Matmata and looked for the village.

24
00:00:54,401 --> 00:00:55,601
No rooftops.

25
00:00:55,713 --> 00:00:56,676
No walls.

26
00:00:56,788 --> 00:00:57,876
No windows.

27
00:00:57,988 --> 00:01:00,564
Just pale yellow hills,
with round holes in them.

28
00:01:00,676 --> 00:01:03,164
My first guess was that
everybody had left.

29
00:01:03,276 --> 00:01:05,764
A ghost town, with very tidy holes.

30
00:01:05,876 --> 00:01:07,201
Then a goat complained.

31
00:01:07,313 --> 00:01:08,138
Below me.

32
00:01:08,251 --> 00:01:10,851
I walked to the edge of the
nearest hole and looked down.

33
00:01:10,964 --> 00:01:15,051
A courtyard, swept clean, with
arched doorways cut into its walls,

34
00:01:15,101 --> 00:01:16,401
and washing on a line.

35
00:01:16,514 --> 00:01:17,964
The village hadn't left.

36
00:01:18,076 --> 00:01:19,764
The village was downstairs.

37
00:01:19,876 --> 00:01:22,126
I had walked straight
over somebody's kitchen.

38
00:01:22,239 --> 00:01:25,364
Amazigh families, also called Berbers,

39
00:01:25,414 --> 00:01:27,639
have lived in homes
like this for centuries.

40
00:01:27,751 --> 00:01:30,189
According to researchers
who study these homes,

41
00:01:30,239 --> 00:01:33,351
the hills went through long
stretches of raids and feuds.

42
00:01:33,463 --> 00:01:36,301
A house nobody can see
is a hard house to rob.

43
00:01:36,870 --> 00:01:38,883
My second guess, I was sure of.

44
00:01:38,995 --> 00:01:40,870
They dug down to hide from the sun.

45
00:01:40,983 --> 00:01:43,445
I'd seen that trick
before, on another trip.

46
00:01:43,557 --> 00:01:44,808
And I was half right.

47
00:01:44,920 --> 00:01:49,295
Researchers measured one of these rooms
on a July day in nineteen ninety-seven.

48
00:01:49,407 --> 00:01:53,545
At three in the afternoon, the air
outside hit forty-six degrees Celsius.

49
00:01:53,657 --> 00:01:56,120
That's about a hundred
and fifteen Fahrenheit.

50
00:01:56,232 --> 00:01:59,857
Inside, the room sat at twenty-seven
or twenty-eight all day.

51
00:01:59,970 --> 00:02:02,758
The ground is a very slow postal service.

52
00:02:02,870 --> 00:02:06,795
Researchers say summer heat takes
months to reach ten metres down,

53
00:02:06,845 --> 00:02:08,270
about thirty-three feet.

54
00:02:08,383 --> 00:02:11,158
By the time it gets there,
summer is long gone.

55
00:02:11,270 --> 00:02:15,120
I lay on my cool bed and unpacked my
whole bag into a niche in the wall.

56
00:02:15,232 --> 00:02:16,270
I was moving in.

57
00:02:16,382 --> 00:02:18,895
I even wrote my new address on a postcard.

58
00:02:19,007 --> 00:02:20,370
Third hole on the left.

59
00:02:20,482 --> 00:02:22,495
Then a small question kept me awake.

60
00:02:22,607 --> 00:02:24,245
Plenty of places are hot.

61
00:02:24,357 --> 00:02:27,432
Why dig here, in these hills,
and not somewhere easier?

62
00:02:28,004 --> 00:02:29,729
On my third day, it rained.

63
00:02:29,842 --> 00:02:30,779
In the desert.

64
00:02:30,891 --> 00:02:32,229
I had packed sunscreen.

65
00:02:32,342 --> 00:02:35,067
I stood under my sun hat while
it filled up like a bowl,

66
00:02:35,117 --> 00:02:37,017
and I felt personally betrayed.

67
00:02:37,129 --> 00:02:40,079
The goat from my first day
watched me from a dry doorway.

68
00:02:40,191 --> 00:02:44,379
These hills only get roughly two hundred
millimetres of rain in a typical year.

69
00:02:44,491 --> 00:02:47,291
That's around eight inches,
and it comes in heavy,

70
00:02:47,354 --> 00:02:48,754
unpredictable bursts.

71
00:02:48,866 --> 00:02:50,442
So I followed the water.

72
00:02:50,554 --> 00:02:53,154
It ran off the bare hilltop
into a narrow gully,

73
00:02:53,204 --> 00:02:56,054
and stopped against a wall of
earth built straight across it.

74
00:02:56,166 --> 00:02:58,779
Behind the wall, a pool of muddy water.

75
00:02:58,892 --> 00:03:01,442
Above that, another wall, and another,

76
00:03:01,529 --> 00:03:02,792
like steps up the valley.

77
00:03:02,904 --> 00:03:04,579
On every step, trees.

78
00:03:04,692 --> 00:03:07,141
Olives, figs, date palms.

79
00:03:07,254 --> 00:03:09,204
These walls are called jessour.

80
00:03:09,317 --> 00:03:12,079
Each one catches the rain
and the soil it carries,

81
00:03:12,129 --> 00:03:13,667
and holds them for the trees.

82
00:03:13,779 --> 00:03:17,679
Some stand taller than five metres,
raised a little after every flood.

83
00:03:17,792 --> 00:03:19,254
The gully is a funnel.

84
00:03:19,366 --> 00:03:21,429
The jessour are the sponge at the bottom.

85
00:03:21,542 --> 00:03:25,366
With them, trees grow here that the
rainfall alone could never support.

86
00:03:25,930 --> 00:03:27,730
That changed how I saw my room.

87
00:03:27,843 --> 00:03:30,343
I'd been thinking of these
pits as hiding places.

88
00:03:30,455 --> 00:03:31,842
They're farmhouses too.

89
00:03:31,955 --> 00:03:34,443
The olive store sits high
in the courtyard wall,

90
00:03:34,568 --> 00:03:36,718
half a floor or more above the ground.

91
00:03:36,830 --> 00:03:40,305
Its roof has an opening, and up
top, the roof is just the hill.

92
00:03:40,418 --> 00:03:44,505
So olives could arrive by camel and be
poured straight down into the store,

93
00:03:44,592 --> 00:03:46,292
without crossing the courtyard.

94
00:03:46,405 --> 00:03:47,880
Meals were cooked down here.

95
00:03:47,993 --> 00:03:49,155
Grain was ground.

96
00:03:49,268 --> 00:03:50,805
Figs were dried in the sun.

97
00:03:50,917 --> 00:03:52,717
The trees live up in the gullies.

98
00:03:52,830 --> 00:03:54,768
The harvest comes down into the hill.

99
00:03:54,880 --> 00:03:57,230
I stood in the middle
and turned around slowly.

100
00:03:57,342 --> 00:03:58,880
The sun is just the weather.

101
00:03:58,993 --> 00:04:00,417
The rain is the landlord.

102
00:04:00,984 --> 00:04:02,797
Which brings me back to my pillow.

103
00:04:02,909 --> 00:04:05,109
How do you dig a house
that doesn't fall on you?

104
00:04:05,222 --> 00:04:06,059
Mostly.

105
00:04:06,171 --> 00:04:08,509
The valleys here are filled with loess,

106
00:04:08,559 --> 00:04:11,222
fine dust laid down by
the wind and packed hard.

107
00:04:11,334 --> 00:04:14,271
In places the layer is ten
to twenty metres thick.

108
00:04:14,384 --> 00:04:18,496
You can dig it with a farm hoe, yet it
stands up in straight walls on its own.

109
00:04:18,609 --> 00:04:20,934
It behaves a lot like packed brown sugar.

110
00:04:21,047 --> 00:04:22,134
Easy to carve.

111
00:04:22,247 --> 00:04:23,334
Holds its shape.

112
00:04:23,446 --> 00:04:24,734
Not a fan of water.

113
00:04:24,846 --> 00:04:27,509
So a family digs a deep, round courtyard,

114
00:04:27,559 --> 00:04:30,109
then carves rooms sideways into its walls.

115
00:04:30,221 --> 00:04:31,959
Almost nothing has to be built.

116
00:04:32,072 --> 00:04:33,096
No bricks.

117
00:04:33,209 --> 00:04:34,234
No beams.

118
00:04:34,346 --> 00:04:37,059
Just a lot of digging, and
somewhere to put the dirt.

119
00:04:37,172 --> 00:04:38,896
I asked for a taller ceiling.

120
00:04:39,009 --> 00:04:40,196
Request denied.

121
00:04:40,309 --> 00:04:42,721
My room kept its low, curved ceiling.

122
00:04:42,834 --> 00:04:44,496
I looked up at it and sneezed.

123
00:04:44,609 --> 00:04:46,497
The ceiling answered with a little dust,

124
00:04:46,547 --> 00:04:47,971
straight onto my pillow.

125
00:04:48,542 --> 00:04:50,280
Next I tried to get a snack.

126
00:04:50,392 --> 00:04:52,429
Grain and olives were stored up high,

127
00:04:52,630 --> 00:04:56,292
reached by footholds cut into the
wall, or by pegs of olive wood.

128
00:04:56,405 --> 00:04:58,592
Like a cookie jar on top of the fridge,

129
00:04:58,642 --> 00:05:00,667
the point was to make it hard to reach.

130
00:05:00,780 --> 00:05:01,604
It was.

131
00:05:01,717 --> 00:05:04,492
I got a couple of footholds
up and slid back down,

132
00:05:04,542 --> 00:05:06,142
gently, on my face.

133
00:05:06,254 --> 00:05:09,742
Animals and farm tools lived in
niches along the entrance tunnel,

134
00:05:09,805 --> 00:05:11,130
away from the bedrooms.

135
00:05:11,242 --> 00:05:13,779
Then I tried the hardest
job, which was water.

136
00:05:13,892 --> 00:05:18,017
A woman who used to live in one of these
homes told the Associated Press about it.

137
00:05:18,130 --> 00:05:21,279
She remembered fetching water
by camel from a distant spring,

138
00:05:21,355 --> 00:05:23,142
and searching for food every day.

139
00:05:23,254 --> 00:05:25,355
I carried a pair of buckets up the tunnel.

140
00:05:25,467 --> 00:05:28,567
Halfway, I sat down and
drank most of the water.

141
00:05:28,679 --> 00:05:30,480
That is why camels were involved.

142
00:05:30,592 --> 00:05:33,030
My room got all its
daylight from the courtyard.

143
00:05:33,142 --> 00:05:36,754
That night, in the dark, I
quietly packed my bag again.

144
00:05:37,324 --> 00:05:39,149
I set my hat down for this part.

145
00:05:39,262 --> 00:05:43,599
In the autumn of nineteen sixty-nine,
rain fell across Tunisia for weeks.

146
00:05:43,711 --> 00:05:47,612
In early October, the coast below these
hills got one hundred to two hundred

147
00:05:47,662 --> 00:05:49,524
millimetres in about six days.

148
00:05:49,636 --> 00:05:52,949
Up to a whole year's rain for
Matmata, in less than a week.

149
00:05:53,062 --> 00:05:57,011
By the end of November, more than five
hundred people had died in Tunisia,

150
00:05:57,061 --> 00:05:59,724
and more than seventy
thousand homes had collapsed.

151
00:05:59,837 --> 00:06:02,474
Around Matmata, water
poured into the pits,

152
00:06:02,524 --> 00:06:04,274
and many of these houses fell in.

153
00:06:04,387 --> 00:06:06,749
Families were offered land in a new town,

154
00:06:06,799 --> 00:06:09,649
New Matmata, for three
dinars a square metre.

155
00:06:09,762 --> 00:06:14,462
The government of President Habib Bourguiba
was building new towns above ground

156
00:06:14,512 --> 00:06:17,649
across the country, and many
families moved into them.

157
00:06:17,762 --> 00:06:20,611
Some of the empty pit houses
slowly fell into ruin.

158
00:06:20,724 --> 00:06:23,974
The rain that kept the trees
alive had brought the walls down.

159
00:06:24,546 --> 00:06:27,159
The next morning I
drove up to New Matmata.

160
00:06:27,271 --> 00:06:28,533
It's a short drive.

161
00:06:28,646 --> 00:06:32,421
One main road, cafés, a
butcher, houses with windows.

162
00:06:32,534 --> 00:06:35,196
In my notebook I drew the
trip as a dotted line,

163
00:06:35,246 --> 00:06:36,821
from the holes to the roofs.

164
00:06:36,934 --> 00:06:39,084
Then I learned the line runs both ways.

165
00:06:39,196 --> 00:06:41,271
Some people who moved into built houses,

166
00:06:41,321 --> 00:06:45,371
researchers found, kept a few rooms
in their family's old pit house,

167
00:06:45,421 --> 00:06:47,258
for the long heat waves of summer.

168
00:06:47,371 --> 00:06:51,471
On a typical summer day, a built house
measured over thirty-six degrees,

169
00:06:51,521 --> 00:06:54,034
and a pit room about
twenty-five and a half.

170
00:06:54,146 --> 00:06:56,871
In winter, the pit room
was the warmer one too.

171
00:06:56,984 --> 00:06:59,484
So some families commute
between two homes.

172
00:06:59,596 --> 00:07:01,571
The commute is measured in degrees.

173
00:07:01,683 --> 00:07:03,046
I tried it myself.

174
00:07:03,158 --> 00:07:05,033
Lunch up top, at a café.

175
00:07:05,146 --> 00:07:08,846
Then a nap downstairs, in the
cool, with my hat over my face.

176
00:07:09,416 --> 00:07:12,841
Back in the old town, I visited
the most famous hole of all.

177
00:07:12,954 --> 00:07:17,678
In nineteen sixty-nine, a family home
there became the Hotel Sidi Driss.

178
00:07:17,791 --> 00:07:22,328
In nineteen seventy-six, a film crew used
it as a location for the first Star Wars

179
00:07:22,378 --> 00:07:23,178
film.

180
00:07:23,041 --> 00:07:27,504
Lucasfilm says a rare storm hit southern
Tunisia soon after filming began there,

181
00:07:27,616 --> 00:07:28,916
and threw the schedule off.

182
00:07:29,028 --> 00:07:31,291
So even the film crew
answered to the rain.

183
00:07:31,403 --> 00:07:33,616
Fans still come to
stand in that courtyard.

184
00:07:33,729 --> 00:07:37,816
Around the town, Reuters found a few
families still living underground in twenty

185
00:07:37,866 --> 00:07:40,728
eighteen, letting visitors
look around for tips.

186
00:07:40,841 --> 00:07:44,341
One woman said leaving her house would
be like throwing away her life and her

187
00:07:44,391 --> 00:07:45,316
traditions.

188
00:07:45,428 --> 00:07:48,954
The same reporters met a man who
still knows how to dig these homes.

189
00:07:49,066 --> 00:07:51,666
His last new one was in
the nineteen seventies.

190
00:07:51,778 --> 00:07:53,566
The visitors came for a film.

191
00:07:53,678 --> 00:07:55,216
The house was here first.

192
00:07:55,329 --> 00:07:57,991
Back in my room, the bag was still packed.

193
00:07:58,104 --> 00:07:59,366
I unzipped it again.

194
00:07:59,950 --> 00:08:02,525
The tray of sand on my
desk still has its hole.

195
00:08:02,638 --> 00:08:04,688
I poured in one spoonful of water.

196
00:08:04,800 --> 00:08:05,862
The walls held.

197
00:08:05,975 --> 00:08:08,600
I poured in a second,
and one side slumped.

198
00:08:08,713 --> 00:08:11,175
That's Matmata, explained by a spoon.

199
00:08:11,288 --> 00:08:14,437
People farm these hills because
the rare rain can be caught,

200
00:08:14,612 --> 00:08:17,012
and they live inside them
because the ground allows it.

201
00:08:17,125 --> 00:08:19,363
The price is that the rain gets a say.

202
00:08:19,475 --> 00:08:23,538
That same digger said he comes back to
repair the houses every time it rains.

203
00:08:23,650 --> 00:08:25,125
I'd want him on speed dial.

204
00:08:25,237 --> 00:08:26,075
WOULD MOVE.

205
00:08:26,187 --> 00:08:28,450
I'd carry the water properly this time,

206
00:08:28,588 --> 00:08:32,488
help mend the walls every autumn, and
keep a bucket of plaster by the bed,

207
00:08:32,600 --> 00:08:34,150
since I wobble into things.

208
00:08:34,263 --> 00:08:37,412
If you like quiet, cool
rooms and don't miss windows,

209
00:08:37,462 --> 00:08:39,050
you'd settle in faster than me.

210
00:08:39,162 --> 00:08:41,800
I pressed the piece from my
pillow into the slumped wall.

211
00:08:41,912 --> 00:08:42,800
It fits.

212
00:08:42,912 --> 00:08:45,788
It still drops a little dust,
like it's thinking it over.

213
00:08:45,900 --> 00:08:47,850
Would you rather fight
the heat every summer,

214
00:08:47,900 --> 00:08:49,863
or fix your walls after every rain?

215
00:08:50,436 --> 00:08:53,324
I set the piece of wall on
a saucer, up on the shelf,

216
00:08:53,374 --> 00:08:54,449
to catch its dust.

217
00:08:54,561 --> 00:08:56,936
My sock from the rainy trip is still damp.

218
00:08:57,049 --> 00:08:58,661
The key still points east.

219
00:08:58,774 --> 00:09:01,649
Where I'm going next, people
make the ground themselves,

220
00:09:01,699 --> 00:09:02,511
by hand.

221
00:09:02,623 --> 00:09:03,661
And it floats.
